@bank all
// SW1 (2026-10-24, patch 0.92.5, the weekend traditions): the Sunday story (sunday-story.trad), read aloud by Chief Holt
// in the junior common room, one chapter each time it's on (the builder picks the chapter in order). Each chapter is said in order,
// line by line. An old adventure book about a slow mail ship; no names, so nothing clashes with the roster. Holt reads
// it plainly; the crew's lines about it are in the tradition's own file.

== story.sunday.1
- Chapter one. The mail ship was the slowest on her route, and proud of it.
- Her pilot was old. Her cook was older. Her engine was older than both of them, and grumpier.
- Every station on the route knew her by her hum. They'd put the kettle on before she docked.
- That trip she carried one letter with no name on it at all. We'll stop there.

== story.sunday.2
- Chapter two. The cook held the letter up to the galley lamp. No name. No station. Just a small green star.
- The pilot said, deliver what you're given. The cook said, to whom? The engine said nothing. Engines rarely do.
- So they asked at every station. Nobody knew the star. Somebody always knew someone who might.
- At the fourth station, a boy said his grandmother drew stars like that. On everything. Next time.

== story.sunday.3
- Chapter three. The grandmother lived at the far end of the last station, in a room full of green stars.
- She looked at the letter a long time. Then she said, that's my handwriting. From forty years ago.
- She'd written it to herself as a girl and posted it, and the post had taken its time. As post does.
- The pilot asked if she'd like to open it. She said, not yet. Have a cup of tea first.

== story.sunday.4
- Chapter four. She opened it after the second cup. Inside was a list of things she hoped she'd do.
- Fly in a ship. Learn to draw. Have a friend who'd sit with her when it was quiet.
- She'd done all three. She read it out, and the cook said he had something in his eye. He hadn't.
- The mail ship went home the long way, because nobody was in a hurry. That's the end of that one.
